Grouped mutate() and filter() compile aggregates and window functions to OVER (PARTITION BY ...). Window functions need an order, so call arrange() first or pass orderBy:.
A function given a PHP closure runs the query so far, loads the rows, and continues in memory, returning a DataFrame. Expressions with no faithful SQL translation do the same. They include round() (R rounds halves to even), first(), last(), nth(), iqr(), cumprod(), consecutive_id(), and median() or quantile() outside PostgreSQL.

filter() keeps rows where every condition is true. Conditions are row closures, vector helpers such as between() or if_any(), or the name of a logical column. NA counts as false. filter_out() drops the rows where every condition is true and keeps rows where a condition is NA, so filter() and filter_out() with the same conditions split a table without overlap.

PHP 8.5+PHP 8.3–8.4


$schools = DataFrame::fromCsv('schools.csv');

// Several conditions are combined with AND; NA counts as false.
echo $schools |&gt;
    filter(fn ($r) =&gt; $r-&gt;prior_score &gt; 75, between('study_hours', 2, 6)) |&gt;
    head(4);

// filter_out() drops the rows where every condition is true.
echo "\n", $schools |&gt; filter_out(fn ($r) =&gt; $r-&gt;prior_score &lt; 70) |&gt; count();


$schools = DataFrame::fromCsv('schools.csv');

// Several conditions are combined with AND; NA counts as false.
echo phplyr($schools)
    -&gt;filter(fn ($r) =&gt; $r-&gt;prior_score &gt; 75, between('study_hours', 2, 6))
    -&gt;head(4);

// filter_out() drops the rows where every condition is true.
echo "\n", phplyr($schools)-&gt;filterOut(fn ($r) =&gt; $r-&gt;prior_score &lt; 70)-&gt;count();



